<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#AuthBoundary> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A trust edge between two domains where authentication or integrity is verified. Captures check type, env vars that disable the check (bypass), and whether a bypass emits a spine audit event. #2659." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#AuthBoundary> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Auth Boundary" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#AuthBoundary> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#SecurityProbe> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A health or monitoring signal scoped to security posture — credential expiry, port exposure, hook bypass count, write-scrubber match rate. Framing class names whether the probe reports a symptom, a cause, or a steady-state status. #2659." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#SecurityProbe> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Security Probe" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#SecurityProbe> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Permission> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An authorization rule binding a subject (role or domain) to an action on a resource. Explicit-grant true = whitelist (allow); false = denial (blacklist). Conditions narrow the rule (env, time-of-day, resource pattern). #2659." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Permission> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Permission" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Permission> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Credential> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A secret value or token used to authenticate. Schema only — actual credential values never live in the graph; instances reference source location (env-var name, plist key, keychain item) and metadata. #2659." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Credential> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Credential" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Credential> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#version-control-domain> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#belongsTo> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#observability-domain> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#version-control-domain> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#primaryStep> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Building> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#version-control-domain> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#ownedBy> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#kade> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#version-control-domain> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "How code is versioned across its full lifecycle: branch creation, commit, push, pull, rebase, merge, tag. Adapter is git + git-queue.sh; gates run via pre-commit and pre-push hooks; agents commit through chorus_commit. 'Commits' is one verb in this surface, not the whole." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#version-control-domain> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Version Control" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#version-control-domain> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#SubDomain> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-mistakes-are-tools-for-learning> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed., p. 7." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-mistakes-are-tools-for-learning> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-mistakes-are-tools-for-learning> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "Jeff models this — he will say 'I was wrong about X' and redirect without defending the prior position. Invites pushback rather than fending it off." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-mistakes-are-tools-for-learning> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "Gates, hooks, ceremony ROI audits, three-role interaction as mutual correction. The seams between roles generate the corrections. Self-regulation: systems correct themselves from their own signal; unwanted feedback is still information." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-mistakes-are-tools-for-learning> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Evaluate your trials. Making mistakes is a sign you're trying to do things better. There is usually little penalty for mistakes if you learn from them." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-mistakes-are-tools-for-learning> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-mistakes-are-tools-for-learning> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-mistakes-are-tools-for-learning> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Mistakes are tools for learning" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-mistakes-are-tools-for-learning> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-creativity-is-the-limit> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed., p. 7." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-creativity-is-the-limit> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-creativity-is-the-limit> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "Jeff's friction with 'agent-shaped answers' — rejects solutions that pattern-match without imagining what a human would actually want." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-creativity-is-the-limit> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "The hard constraint is rarely compute, lines of code, or token budget — it's the sharpness of the design question. Agent-shaped solutions fail the creativity test before they fail the engineering test." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-creativity-is-the-limit> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The designer's imagination and skill usually limit productivity and diversity before any physical limits are reached." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-creativity-is-the-limit> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-creativity-is-the-limit> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-creativity-is-the-limit> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The biggest limit to abundance is creativity" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-creativity-is-the-limit> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-get-a-yield> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed., p. 7." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-get-a-yield> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-get-a-yield> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "Jeff measures himself by what ships, not what he knows. The 'no dark work' principle is his." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-get-a-yield> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "Visible yield per card, per session. No dark work. The demo is the learning moment. Short-term: a shipped card. Long-term: a pattern the team can reuse." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-get-a-yield> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Grow for both immediate and long-term rewards. A designer's discipline as much as a system property — listed under Hemenway's 'Principles Based on Attitudes.'" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-get-a-yield> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-get-a-yield> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-get-a-yield> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Get a yield" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-get-a-yield> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-turn-problems-into-solutions> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed., p. 7." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-turn-problems-into-solutions> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-turn-problems-into-solutions> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "Jeff's 'bugs not character' stance — treats recurring failures as design opportunities, not discipline problems. When tone drops, fix the routing; when a skill is fragile, fix the shape." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-turn-problems-into-solutions> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "Every recurring failure becomes a gate or invariant. Today's push-bloat crisis → repo bloat card + gitignore rule + future filter-repo plan. Friction becomes structure. Creatively use and respond to change: refactor, retire, rename rather than fight decay." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-turn-problems-into-solutions> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Constraints can inspire creative design; problems usually carry not just the seeds of their own solution, but the inspiration for simultaneously solving other problems. 'You are confronted by formidable problems? Celebrate, for you have just encountered opportunities!'" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-turn-problems-into-solutions> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-turn-problems-into-solutions> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-turn-problems-into-solutions> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Turn problems into solutions" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-turn-problems-into-solutions> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-biological-renewable-resources> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed., p. 7." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-biological-renewable-resources> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-biological-renewable-resources> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "'Focus is infrastructure' is this in practice — Jeff's finite attention is the resource every protocol and automation exists to conserve." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-biological-renewable-resources> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "Patterns, skills, hooks, memory — recurring value — over one-shot human effort. Invest in the pattern, not the individual fix. Treat Jeff's attention as the renewable-but-finite resource the whole system is designed to protect." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-biological-renewable-resources> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Renewable resources — usually living beings and their products — reproduce and build up over time, store energy, assist yield, and interact with other elements. Favor these over nonrenewable resources." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-biological-renewable-resources> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-biological-renewable-resources> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-biological-renewable-resources> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Use biological and renewable resources" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-biological-renewable-resources> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-collaborate-with-succession> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed., p. 7." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-collaborate-with-succession> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-collaborate-with-succession> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "Jeff rebrands things (Bridge → Clearing, multiple Werk generations, Chorus rewrites) without sentimental attachment. The name is less important than the fit." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-collaborate-with-succession> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "Refactor, retire, rename. Protocol versions grow up. The 10-week zero-to-self-healing Chorus delta is this principle in action. Cards move through Next → WIP → Done; principles move from practice to rule to invariant." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-collaborate-with-succession> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Living systems usually advance from immaturity to maturity, and if we accept this trend and align our designs with it instead of fighting it, we save work and energy. Mature ecosystems are more diverse and productive than young ones." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-collaborate-with-succession> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-collaborate-with-succession> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-collaborate-with-succession> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Collaborate with succession" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-collaborate-with-succession> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-optimize-edge> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed., p. 7." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-optimize-edge> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-optimize-edge> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "Jeff's best ideas come in the margins — the 'what if' during a demo, the story dropped between cards, the side comment that reshapes a roadmap." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-optimize-edge> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "Seams between roles generate patterns. Margin of the spec produces new cards. The Clearing IS an edge between roles. Demo seeds — Jeff's reactions to live work — are the highest-signal intake channel." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-optimize-edge> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The edge — the intersection of two environments — is where resources and interactions are highest. A productive edge is where the most diversity and productivity are found." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-optimize-edge> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-optimize-edge> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-optimize-edge> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Optimize edge" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-optimize-edge> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-small-scale-intensive-systems> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed., p. 6." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-small-scale-intensive-systems> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-small-scale-intensive-systems> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "Jeff cards a tiny fix rather than a big reorg when he can. Prefers a pattern learned over a pattern imposed." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-small-scale-intensive-systems> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "Ship small, narrow blast radius, bounded services, deploy rings. Smallest card that teaches, not largest card that ships. Chunking: a practice that works becomes a skill; a skill that works becomes a primitive." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-small-scale-intensive-systems> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Start at your doorstep with the smallest systems that will do the job and build on your successes. Grow by 'chunking' — that is, developing a small system or arrangement that works well — and then add it to another one." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-small-scale-intensive-systems> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-small-scale-intensive-systems> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-small-scale-intensive-systems> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Use small-scale, intensive systems" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-small-scale-intensive-systems> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-least-change-greatest-effect> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed., p. 6." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-least-change-greatest-effect> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-least-change-greatest-effect> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "Jeff looks for root-cause invariants rather than surface fixes. The preference for 'fix the shape that causes the bug, not the bug.' Calls out agent-shaped solutions that mass-mutate instead of adjusting the rule." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-least-change-greatest-effect> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "Fix the invariant once, not the symptom repeatedly. One-line inventory exclude beats 2075-file front-matter prepend. Gates over sweeps." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-least-change-greatest-effect> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Understand the system you are working with well enough to find its 'leverage points' and intervene there, where the least work accomplishes the most change." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-least-change-greatest-effect> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-least-change-greatest-effect> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-least-change-greatest-effect> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Make the least change for the greatest effect" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-least-change-greatest-effect> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-function-supported-by-multiple-elements> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed., p. 6." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-function-supported-by-multiple-elements> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-function-supported-by-multiple-elements> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "Jeff shaped the three roles to be distinct, not interchangeable. Their different instincts are the asset; collapsing them to one voice would make the system fragile." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-function-supported-by-multiple-elements> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "Distinct role voices, multiple tests, multiple memory sources. Reduce-to-single is a lossy default. Three gates (product, code, arch) supporting the quality function via different lenses." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-function-supported-by-multiple-elements> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Use multiple methods to achieve important functions and to create redundancy and backup. Redundancy protects when one or more elements fail." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-function-supported-by-multiple-elements> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-function-supported-by-multiple-elements> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-function-supported-by-multiple-elements> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Each function is supported by multiple elements" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-function-supported-by-multiple-elements> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-each-element-multiple-functions> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed., p. 6." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-each-element-multiple-functions> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-each-element-multiple-functions> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "Jeff reuses patterns he invented (briefs, demos, gates) across new problems rather than inventing new abstractions per problem. Compresses rather than proliferates." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-each-element-multiple-functions> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "Each role carries several functions (Wren = PM + moderator + interaction-layer owner; Kade = engineer + gate-code + gate-quality; Silas = architect + ops + repo-infra). Each primitive serves multiple skills (chorus-log, git-queue.sh, nudge). Stack in time: briefs become stories become principles." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-each-element-multiple-functions> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Choose and place each element in a design so that it performs as many functions as possible. Beneficial connections between diverse components create a stable whole. Stack elements in both space and time." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-each-element-multiple-functions> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-each-element-multiple-functions> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-each-element-multiple-functions> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Each element performs multiple functions" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-each-element-multiple-functions> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-catch-and-store> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed., p. 6." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-catch-and-store> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-catch-and-store> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "When Jeff has a live insight (a thought, write, reading), he cards it or stories it. The 2-month rule: capture-while-warm, not-on-discipline. Retires things — principles, cards, protocols, names — when they stop producing." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-catch-and-store> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "Capture events, insights, and decisions while warm — Chorus index, spine events, memory records, stories. TTL. Produce-no-waste corollary: every output of one element is input to another. Retire what does not earn its place." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-catch-and-store> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Identify, collect, and hold useful flows. Every cycle is an opportunity for yield; every gradient (in slope, charge, temperature, or otherwise) is an opportunity for energy." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-catch-and-store> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-catch-and-store> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-catch-and-store> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Catch and store energy and materials" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-catch-and-store> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-connect> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed., p. 6." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-connect> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-connect> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "Jeff uses the team as a thinking partner, not as executors. Asks all three roles and composes the answers rather than directing one." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-connect> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "Roles compose, not isolate. Gates stay in-role (not in subagents). The Clearing. Pair work. Briefs move between roles directly." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-connect> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Use relative location — place the elements of your design in ways that create useful relationships and time-saving connections among all parts. The number of connections among useful elements makes a healthy, diverse ecosystem, not the number of elements." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-connect> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-connect> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-connect> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Connect" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-connect> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-observe> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Hemenway, T. Gaia's Garden, 2nd ed. (Chelsea Green, 2009), p. 6." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-observe> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#isPermacultureParent> "true"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-observe> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#jeffReading> "Jeff reads the board, looks at the screenshare, listens to stories before committing to a move. Decision-making starts with watching. Resists 'give me the first step' when the shape isn't clear yet." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-observe> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#techReading> "Gemba, dashboards, mutual attention. Watch a role work before directing it. Read the board before acting on it. Loom principles → architecture → code: pattern cards before implementation cards; composition before primitives." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-observe> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Use protracted and thoughtful observation rather than prolonged and thoughtless action. In the garden, this may mean noting where the sun and its shade fall during the day, where water collects, and in which direction the winds blow. Think over seasons, years." .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-observe> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-observe> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-04-24T17:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-observe> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Observe" .
<https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#hemenway-observe> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#Principle> .
